Well, aye, there was a certain irony to my dissenting response; but I see t=
he purpose of rational argument as being to seek a mutual understanding, ei=
ther to reach a common set of conclusions, when all parties share the same =
axioms, or to achieve a clear understanding of one another's axioms when th=
e parties' axioms differ. The focus of discussion is naturally on the diffe=
rences of views, because it's in dealing with those differences that discus=
sion is needed. But it's also true that when I discuss with my students, ra=
ther than my peers and colleagues, I do much more as you would recommend, s=
ince the goal is to foster the student's development, not to seek a mutual =
understanding. (On Lojban-list I've always felt myself to be among peers.)

I do acknowledge, though, that many newcomers might be discouraged in the w=
ay you say they are, so my disagreement is more with the essay you asked us=
 to read than with your sensible advice about how newcomers should be treat=
ed.

